WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.960
Nine at night, middle of calving.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.650
There's a new relief
milker starting Monday,

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:10.490
and your notes on the shed routine
are half a page of scribble.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.240
This is where an AI chat
tool earns its keep.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.460
ChatGPT, Copilot, Gemini or Claude.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.810
Type or say your rough notes,

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.960
and ask for a short, friendly text.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.530
A few seconds later you've
got a clear message,

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:26.200
every fact from your notes,

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.430
and a version in their first
language if you need it.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:32.660
Rosters, meeting notes, a
summary of a long contract:

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.060
same idea.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.300
Here's the catch.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.300
Your note for the fencing
contractor says Tuesday would suit.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.560
Nothing's booked.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:41.870
The draft comes back:

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.370
"Just confirming you're
booked in for 8 am Tuesday."

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.360
It reads fine.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.660
But nobody booked anything,
and nobody said 8 am.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.700
The tool wrote what
contractor texts usually say.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.240
Send it, and Tom turns up for
a job he never agreed to.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.120
So before anything goes out, put
the draft beside your notes.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.760
Check every date, name,
number and promise.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.610
If you can't point
to it in your notes,

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.000
take it out or turn
it into a question.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.960
Let it write the first draft.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.810
You check it, and you send it.
